{"name":"Seeress","hex":"#a99ba9","fetched":"2026-10-13","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"seeress","whole":true,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A female seer, soothsayer or diviner"]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/seeress"}],"entries":[{"title":"Seeress (Germanic)","blocks":[{"text":"In Germanic paganism, a seeress is a woman said to have the ability to foretell future events and perform sorcery. They are also referred to with many other names meaning \"prophetess\", \"staff bearer\" and \"sorceress\", and they are frequently called witches both in early sources and in modern scholarship. In Norse mythology the seeress is usually referred to as völva or vala."}],"link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Seeress_(Germanic)","wikidata":"Q1477129"},{"title":"Divination","blocks":[{"text":"Divination is the attempt to gain insight into a question or situation by way of a magic ritual or practice. Using various methods, throughout history, diviners have been providing answers to querents by reading signs, events, or omens, often claiming to receive insight through supernatural agents such as spirits, gods, god-like-beings or the \"will of the universe\"."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/d2/Rhumsiki_crab_sorceror.jpg/330px-Rhumsiki_crab_sorceror.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Divination","wikidata":"Q1043197"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Vell%C3%A9da_jardin_luxembourg.JPG/500px-Vell%C3%A9da_jardin_luxembourg.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/4/47/Vell%C3%A9da_jardin_luxembourg.JPG/500px-Vell%C3%A9da_jardin_luxembourg.JPG?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Sculpture of the Germanic seeress Veleda, by Hippolyte Maindron, 1844, in Jardin du Luxembourg, Paris.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AVell%C3%A9da_jardin_luxembourg.JPG"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/0/08/Nornorna_vid_Urdarbrunnen.jpg/500px-Nornorna_vid_Urdarbrunnen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/en/thumb/0/08/Nornorna_vid_Urdarbrunnen.jpg/500px-Nornorna_vid_Urdarbrunnen.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The fate of men was always in the hands of female powers. An illustration of the Norns who spin the threads of fate at the foot of Yggdrasil, the tree of the world. Beneath them is the well Urðarbrunnr with the two swans that have engendered all the swans in the world.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ANornorna_vid_Urdarbrunnen.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Lune_Grand_Palais_-_Velleda._Effet_de_lune_-_Jules_Lenepveu_-_without_border.jpg/500px-Lune_Grand_Palais_-_Velleda._Effet_de_lune_-_Jules_Lenepveu_-_without_border.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Lune_Grand_Palais_-_Velleda._Effet_de_lune_-_Jules_Lenepveu_-_without_border.jpg/1280px-Lune_Grand_Palais_-_Velleda._Effet_de_lune_-_Jules_Lenepveu_-_without_border.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Lune_Grand_Palais_-_Velleda._Effet_de_lune_-_Jules_Lenepveu_-_without_border.jpg/500px-Lune_Grand_Palais_-_Velleda._Effet_de_lune_-_Jules_Lenepveu_-_without_border.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"The seeress Veleda as painted by Jules Eugène Lenepveu, 1883","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ALune_Grand_Palais_-_Velleda._Effet_de_lune_-_Jules_Lenepveu_-_without_border.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/c